-- title:
--   Compatible Atkin–Lehner automorphisms of K₁ supseteq K₂
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, $M \ge 1$ a natural number with $p \nmid M$, and $L$ a characteristic-zero field which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ for the set $\{p\}$. Let $K_1$ and $K_2$ be intermediate fields of $L \subseteq L((q))$ (Laurent series over $L$) subject to: $K_1$ is the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the image, under the coefficientwise map [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to L$, of [`ModularCurve.x1x0FunctionFieldC ℚ M p`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L142), that is of the $\mathbb{Q}$-subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by all quotients $P_f/P_g$ of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms $f,g$ of a common weight for $\Gamma_1(M) \cap \Gamma_0(p)$ with $P_g \ne 0$; and $K_2$ is the analogous $L$-subfield attached to $\Gamma_0(Mp)$. Let $\iota_2 : K_2 \to K_1$ be a ring homomorphism which is the identity on underlying Laurent series, and let $j \in K_1$ be the element whose Laurent series is the image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), the $q$-expansion $q^{-1} \cdot j\mathrm{Num}$ of the $j$-invariant. Then there exist $L$-algebra automorphisms $\sigma$ of $K_1$ and $\sigma_2$ of $K_2$ such that the Laurent series of $\sigma(j)$ is the image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of [`ModularCurve.qExpand ℚ p`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25) applied to [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), i.e. of $j$ with all exponents multiplied by $p$, and such that for every $y \in K_2$ the Laurent series of $\sigma(\iota_2 y)$ and of $\sigma_2(y)$ coincide.
--
--   This is the Atkin–Lehner involution $W_p$ in its two incarnations, on the $q$-expansion function field of $X(\Gamma_1(M) \cap \Gamma_0(p))$ and on that of $X_0(Mp)$, packaged as a compatible pair of automorphisms normalising the inclusion $K_2 \subseteq K_1$ and sending $j(q)$ to $j(q^p)$. It is used downstream in the comparison of residue fields and ramification data at the Gauss valuation on these function fields, and in the identification of the kernel of the relevant reduction map.
